   Denies alcohol or illicit drug use.\X0A\\X0A\## Family History:\X0A\\X0A\*   Non-contributory\X0A\\X0A\## Review of Systems (ROS):\X0A\\X0A\*   **General:** Weakness, fatigue.\X0A\*   **HEENT:** Denies headache, visual changes, hearing loss, nasal congestion (resolved from URI).\X0A\*   **Respiratory:** Shortness of breath, non-productive cough.\X0A\*   **Cardiovascular:** Denies chest pain, palpitations, edema.\X0A\*   **Gastrointestinal:** Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain.\X0A\*   **Genitourinary:** Denies dysuria, frequency, hematuria.\X0A\*   **Neurological:** Denies headache, dizziness, weakness, numbness.\X0A\*   **Musculoskeletal:** Denies joint pain, muscle pain.\X0A\*   **Skin:** Denies rash, itching.\X0A\*   **Psychiatric:** Denies depression, anxiety.\X0A\\X0A\## Physical Examination:\X0A\\X0A\*   **General:** Alert and oriented, appears mildly distressed due to shortness of breath.\X0A\*   **Vitals:** HR 98 bpm, BP 130/80 mmHg, SpO2 92% on room air, Temp 37.2°C (99.0°F).\X0A\*   **HEENT:** Normocephalic, atraumatic. Mucous membranes moist. No pharyngeal erythema or exudates.\X0A\*   **Neck:** Supple, no lymphadenopathy or thyroid enlargement.\X0A\*   **Lungs:** Auscultation reveals decreased breath sounds at the right lung base. No wheezes, rhonchi, or crackles are heard. Percussion is dull over the right lower lobe.\X0A\*   **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. No peripheral edema.\X0A\*   **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, non-distended. Bowel sounds present in all four quadrants.\X0A\*   **Neurological:** Grossly intact.\X0A\*   **Skin:** Warm, dry, no rashes or lesions.\X0A\\X0A\## Investigations:\X0A\\X0A\*   **Chest X-ray:** Shows evidence of atelectasis in the right lower lobe. No evidence of consolidation or pleural effusion.\X0A\\X0A\## Assessment:\X0A\\X0A\1.  **Atelectasis (Right Lower Lobe):** Likely post-obstructive secondary to mucus plugging following a recent URI. This explains the decreased breath sounds, dullness to percussion, and shortness of breath. The patient's SpO2 of 92% suggests some degree of ventilation-perfusion mismatch.\X0A\\X0A\## Plan:\X0A\\X0A\1.  **Oxygen Therapy:** Initiate supplemental oxygen via nasal cannula to maintain SpO2 > 94%.\X0A\2.  **Respiratory Therapy:** Consult respiratory therapy for chest physiotherapy (percussion and postural drainage) and incentive spirometry to help mobilize secretions and re-expand the collapsed lung tissue.\X0A\3.  **Bronchodilators:** Consider nebulized albuterol/ipratropium if there is any suspicion of underlying bronchospasm, although wheezing was not noted on exam.\X0A\4.  **Hydration:** Encourage oral fluids to help thin secretions.\X0A\5.  **Monitoring:** Continuous pulse oximetry monitoring. Frequent reassessment of respiratory status and vital signs.\X0A\6.  **Further Investigations:** If symptoms do not improve with initial management, consider CT chest to rule out other causes of obstruction (e.g., foreign body, tumor) or complications.\X0A\7.  **Disposition:** Admit to the hospital for monitoring and treatment of atelectasis.